-- title:
--   Lemma 2.7: Abelian ideals are central and ideals inside V vanish
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak g$ be admissible and fix its structural presentation with module subspace $V$. For every Lie ideal $\mathfrak a$ of $\mathfrak g$,
--
--   $$[\mathfrak a,\mathfrak a]=0\Longrightarrow\mathfrak a\subseteq\mathfrak z(\mathfrak g),\qquad \mathfrak a\subseteq V\Longrightarrow\mathfrak a=0.$$
--
--   The first clause is intrinsic, while the second uses the specified module complement. These assertions rule out nonzero invariant edges supported in $V$.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 5, Lemma 2.7: Abelian ideals are central and ideals inside V vanish

